| $T_k$ | Length of strategy $k$'s OOS window, **in years**. |

Unobserved: the true Sharpe $S_k$. All variances below are **cross-sectional** — dispersion *across the $K$ strategies*,
not time-series variance of any one strategy's pnl.

## The dispersion ledger (read this first — everything else is bookkeeping on it)

Three cross-sectional dispersions, and how they relate:

$$
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_k\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k\big)}_{V\ \text{: observed OOS spread}}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_k\big(S_k\big)}_{\tau^2\ \text{: true spread}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\overline{1/T_k}}_{\text{measurement noise}}
$$

- $V$ — how differently your strategies *appear* to have performed live. Directly computable.
- $\tau^2$ — how differently they *truly* perform. Not observable per strategy, but its total is: $\tau^2 = V - \overline{1/T_k}$,
  because each observed OOS Sharpe scatters around its own truth with variance $1/T_k$, and independent variances add.
- $\overline{1/T_k}$ — pure yardstick noise: the part of the observed spread that exists only because Sharpes are measured
  over finite windows.

The true spread $\tau^2$ then splits again, by what the backtest can and cannot see:

$$
\underbrace{\tau^2}_{\text{true spread}}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_k\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k\big)}_{\text{explained by IS (via the line)}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\sigma^2}_{\text{invisible to IS}}
$$

- The first piece is the dispersion of the fitted values $\alpha + \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ — the portion of genuine
  strategy-quality differences that the backtest successfully predicts.
- $\sigma^2$ — the portion it cannot: how much true Sharpes still differ **among strategies with identical backtests**.
  This is the method's central quantity. Everything the pipeline computes exists to isolate it.

Stacking both decompositions, the observed OOS spread has exactly three sources:

$$
V \;=\; \underbrace{\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_k(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}})}_{\text{IS-explained truth}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sigma^2}_{\text{IS-invisible truth}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\overline{1/T_k}}_{\text{measurement noise}},
$$

and the regression $R^2$ is the first piece over $V$. This shows why raw $R^2$ *understates* the backtest: the denominator
contains measurement noise that nothing could ever explain. The honest question is not "what fraction of $V$ does IS explain"
but "what fraction of $\tau^2$" — and the pipeline answers it by removing the noise term before pricing anything.

**Information flow, in words:** the backtest's job is to predict *true* dispersion. Of the observed OOS spread, first strip
the yardstick noise (it carries no information about anything); of what remains — genuine quality differences — the line
captures $\beta^2\operatorname{Var}(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}})$ and misses $\sigma^2$. The missed part is exactly what only live
trading can reveal, which is why $1/\sigma^2$ becomes the exchange rate between backtest evidence and live years.

## The slope as attenuation coefficient

The regression slope is, definitionally,

$$
\beta \;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_k\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k,\ \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_k\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k\big)}
\;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_k\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k,\ S_k\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_k\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k\big)},
$$

the second equality because OOS measurement noise is uncorrelated with the backtest — so the slope automatically targets
**truth**, not the noisy OOS print. It is an *attenuation coefficient*: the fraction of an IS deviation that survives
into expected truth. If a strategy's backtest sits one Sharpe unit above the book, expect its true Sharpe only $\beta$
units above.

Why $\beta < 1$ — the errors-in-variables reading. Cross-sectional dispersion in IS Sharpes has two sources: genuine
quality differences (signal) and everything else — IS sampling noise, overfitting, path selection (noise, in the sense of
"uninformative about truth"). In the classical unit-loading model $\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}} = S + \eta$,

$$
\beta = \frac{\tau^2}{\tau^2 + \omega^2}
= \frac{\text{signal dispersion}}{\text{signal dispersion} + \text{noise dispersion}}
\;\in\; (0,1),
$$

i.e. the slope *is* the signal-to-total-variance ratio of the backtest as a predictor. $\beta = 0.4$ means 60% of the
cross-sectional spread in your backtests is dispersion that carries no forward information — and regression to the mean
by exactly that fraction is not pessimism but arithmetic. (Caveat: the identity above assumes IS is an unbiased
unit-loading measurement, which selection and median compression can break — the data may even show
$\operatorname{Var}(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}) < \tau^2$, impossible under that model. The general definition
$\beta = \operatorname{Cov}/\operatorname{Var}$ needs no model and is the one the pipeline uses; the
signal-to-noise reading is intuition, not an estimator.)

Attenuation composes: each independent noise layer multiplies a further factor into the slope. More parameters tried per
strategy, more path selection, shorter IS windows — each shrinks $\beta$. This is why $\beta$ is the single most
informative diagnostic of a research process: it is the fraction of backtest differentiation that survives contact with
reality, measured on your own book.

