## Dispersion accounting of the estimator itself

The estimator's cross-sectional spread is exactly the spread that calibrated estimates must have. Its dispersion,

$$
\operatorname{Var}_k\big(\hat S^{\text{fwd}}\big)
= \underbrace{\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_k\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}_{\text{IS-explained truth, kept in full}}
+ \underbrace{\sigma^2\,\frac{T_k\,\sigma^2}{1+T_k\,\sigma^2}}_{\text{IS-invisible truth, kept in proportion to live evidence}},
$$

matches $\operatorname{Var}_k\big(\mathbb{E}[S_k \mid \text{data}]\big)$ term by term: the estimator keeps *all* the
dispersion the backtest legitimately explains, admits the IS-invisible dispersion only to the degree the live record has
resolved it, and keeps *none* of the measurement noise. Shrinking further would under-disperse (underrating genuinely
good strategies); shrinking less would let noise masquerade as differentiation. This is the sense in which no additional
regularization belongs: the dispersion books balance.

