## Weighted moments (used everywhere)

Let $\tilde w_k$ be the normalized regression weights ($\sum_k \tilde w_k = 1$; pilot: $\tilde w_k \propto T_k$,
final: $\tilde w_k \propto (\sigma^2 + 1/T_k)^{-1}$). For any $x_k, y_k$:

$$
\bar x_w = \sum_k \tilde w_k\, x_k,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}_w(x) = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,(x_k - \bar x_w)^2,
\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}_w(x, y) = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,(x_k - \bar x_w)(y_k - \bar y_w).
$$

Every variance, covariance, mean, $R^2$, and noise-average in this document uses **the same weights as the regression
pass it belongs to**. This is substantive, not cosmetic: the decompositions below are exact algebraic identities of the
fit only under one consistent weighting, and the weighted noise term $\sum_k \tilde w_k / T_k$ genuinely differs from the
unweighted mean of $1/T_k$ when windows are heterogeneous (Jensen: the unweighted average of reciprocals is larger) —
mixing conventions silently biases $\sigma^2$ and hence the credibility assigned to backtests.

## The dispersion ledger (read this first — everything else is bookkeeping on it)

Three cross-sectional dispersions, and how they relate:

$$
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}_{V\ \text{: observed OOS spread}}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(S\big)}_{\tau^2\ \text{: true spread}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}}_{\text{measurement noise}}
$$

- $V$ — how differently your strategies *appear* to have performed live. Directly computable.
- $\tau^2$ — how differently they *truly* perform. Not observable per strategy, but its total is:
  $\tau^2 = V - \sum_k \tilde w_k / T_k$, because each observed OOS Sharpe scatters around its own truth with variance
  $1/T_k$, and independent variances add.
- $\sum_k \tilde w_k / T_k$ — pure yardstick noise: the part of the observed spread that exists only because Sharpes are
  measured over finite windows.

The true spread $\tau^2$ then splits again, by what the backtest can and cannot see:

$$
\underbrace{\tau^2}_{\text{true spread}}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}_{\text{explained by IS (via the line)}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\sigma^2}_{\text{invisible to IS}}
$$

- The first piece is the dispersion of the fitted values $\alpha + \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ — the portion of genuine
  strategy-quality differences that the backtest successfully predicts.
- $\sigma^2$ — the portion it cannot: how much true Sharpes still differ **among strategies with identical backtests**.
  This is the method's central quantity. Everything the pipeline computes exists to isolate it.

Stacking both decompositions, the observed OOS spread has exactly three sources:

$$
V \;=\; \underbrace{\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}_{\text{IS-explained truth}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sigma^2}_{\text{IS-invisible truth}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}}_{\text{measurement noise}},
\qquad
R^2 = \frac{\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}{V}.
$$

This shows why raw $R^2$ *understates* the backtest: the denominator contains measurement noise that nothing could ever
explain. The honest question is not "what fraction of $V$ does IS explain" but "what fraction of $\tau^2$" — and the
pipeline answers it by removing the noise term before pricing anything.

**Information flow, in words:** the backtest's job is to predict *true* dispersion. Of the observed OOS spread, first
strip the yardstick noise (it carries no information about anything); of what remains — genuine quality differences —
the line captures $\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}})$ and misses $\sigma^2$. The missed part is exactly
what only live trading can reveal, which is why $1/\sigma^2$ becomes the exchange rate between backtest evidence and
live years.

## The slope as attenuation coefficient

The regression slope is, definitionally,

$$
\beta \;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}
\;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ S\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)},
$$

the second equality because OOS measurement noise is uncorrelated with the backtest — so the slope automatically targets
**truth**, not the noisy OOS print. It is an *attenuation coefficient*: the fraction of an IS deviation that survives
into expected truth. If a strategy's backtest sits one Sharpe unit above the book, expect its true Sharpe only $\beta$
units above.

Why $\beta < 1$ — the errors-in-variables reading. Cross-sectional dispersion in IS Sharpes has two sources: genuine
quality differences (signal) and everything else — IS sampling noise, overfitting, path selection (noise, in the sense
of "uninformative about truth"). In the classical unit-loading model $\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}} = S + \eta$,

$$
\beta = \frac{\tau^2}{\tau^2 + \omega^2}
= \frac{\text{signal dispersion}}{\text{signal dispersion} + \text{noise dispersion}}
\;\in\; (0,1),
$$

i.e. the slope *is* the signal-to-total-variance ratio of the backtest as a predictor. $\beta = 0.4$ means 60% of the
cross-sectional spread in your backtests is dispersion


# Forward-Looking Sharpe Estimation via Two-Pass Weighted Regression

## Inputs

For each strategy $k = 1, \dots, K$:

| Symbol | Definition |
|---|---|
| $\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ | $\sqrt{252}\cdot\operatorname{median}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{IS}}_k)\,/\,\operatorname{std}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{IS}}_k)$ — annualized in-sample Sharpe. Median (not mean) because selection on favorable paths creates spurious positive skew in IS pnl; the median's bounded influence immunizes against it. |
| $\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$ | $\sqrt{252}\cdot\operatorname{mean}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k)\,/\,\operatorname{std}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k)$ — annualized out-of-sample Sharpe. |
| $T_k$ | Length of strategy $k$'s OOS window, **in years**. |

Unobserved: the true Sharpe $S_k$. All dispersions below are **cross-sectional** — spread *across the $K$ strategies*,
not time-series variance of any one strategy's pnl.

## The measurement model

Both observed Sharpes are noisy views of the same truth, but they are *not* symmetric:

$$
\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \;=\; S_k \;+\; b_k \;+\; \eta_k,
\qquad\qquad
\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \;=\; S_k \;+\; \nu_k,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\nu_k) = \frac{1}{T_k}.
$$

- $\eta_k$ — IS sampling noise (finite backtest window, pnl randomness).
- $\nu_k$ — OOS sampling noise, with **known** variance $1/T_k$: the live window's length is the yardstick.
- $b_k$ — the overfitting/selection bias, and it is **strategy-dependent**: it grows with the number of variants tried,
  the luck of the selected path, the parameter count, the aggressiveness of median compression for that pnl shape.
  A constant bias was never the honest model.
- Crucially, $b_k$ appears **only in the IS equation.** The live period never passed through the selection machine,
  so OOS is unbiased: $\mathbb{E}[\nu_k \mid S_k] = 0$. This single asymmetry drives the entire design.

### Decomposing the bias

Any cross-sectional random quantity splits into a mean, a truth-correlated part, and an orthogonal remainder:

$$
b_k \;=\; \underbrace{\bar b}_{\text{average bias}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\gamma\,\big(S_k - \bar S\big)}_{\text{truth-correlated part}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\xi_k}_{\text{idiosyncratic part},\ \perp S}.
$$

Substituting back, the IS equation becomes a **scaled** measurement:

$$
\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k
= \big(\bar b - \gamma\,\bar S\big)
\;+\; \underbrace{(1+\gamma)}_{=\,a\ \text{(loading)}}\,S_k
\;+\; \underbrace{\eta_k + \xi_k}_{\text{total IS noise}} .
$$

**Selection makes $\gamma < 0$.** Strategies that survived selection despite weak truth are exactly those whose bias
was large (they needed the luck); strong-truth strategies survived on modest luck. Conditioning on survival therefore
builds in $\operatorname{Cov}(b_k, S_k) < 0$ — at the selection margin, bias substitutes for quality. Hence the loading
$a = 1+\gamma < 1$: the IS statistic responds to a one-unit change in truth with less than one unit, and its
cross-sectional spread $a^2\tau^2 + \operatorname{Var}(\eta+\xi)$ can even fall **below** the true spread $\tau^2$.
(Under a constant-bias model that would be impossible — added independent noise only widens a distribution. If your
data shows $\operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}) < \tau^2$, that is not a paradox but the signature of
truth-anticorrelated selection bias.)

### How the pipeline absorbs $b_k$ without ever modeling it

The three components of the bias map one-to-one onto the three regression outputs:

| Component of $b_k$ | Its effect on IS | Absorbed by |
|---|---|---|
| $\bar b$ — average inflation | shifts the IS mean | $\alpha$ (intercept recenters onto the OOS anchor) |
| $\gamma\,(S_k-\bar S)$ — truth-correlated tilt | rescales the loading to $a = 1+\gamma$ | $\beta$ (slope adapts to whatever $a$ is) |
| $\xi_k$ — idiosyncratic scatter | extra noise on IS | flattens $\beta$ further and inflates $\sigma^2$ — its damage is *measured* and charged to the backtest's credibility |

Nothing about $b_k$ needs to be known, estimated, or corrected per strategy: its mean is killed by the intercept, its
systematic tilt by the slope, and its scatter is priced into $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$. This is the content of
"the regression is the prior." (The one escape hatch: if part of $\xi_k$ is *predictable* from observables — e.g. the
number of trials $n_k$ — leaving it in the noise wastes information; add the observable as a regressor and that part
migrates from "noise" to "explained." See the multivariate note at the end.)

## Weighted moments (used everywhere)

Let $\tilde w_k$ be the normalized regression weights ($\sum_k \tilde w_k = 1$; pilot: $\tilde w_k \propto T_k$,
final: $\tilde w_k \propto (\sigma^2 + 1/T_k)^{-1}$). For any $x_k, y_k$:

$$
\bar x_w = \sum_k \tilde w_k\, x_k,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}_w(x) = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,(x_k - \bar x_w)^2,
\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}_w(x, y) = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,(x_k - \bar x_w)(y_k - \bar y_w).
$$

Every variance, covariance, mean, $R^2$, and noise-average in this document uses **the same weights as the regression
pass it belongs to**. This is substantive, not cosmetic: the decompositions below are exact algebraic identities of the
fit only under one consistent weighting, and the weighted noise term $\sum_k \tilde w_k / T_k$ genuinely differs from
the unweighted mean of $1/T_k$ when windows are heterogeneous (Jensen: the unweighted average of reciprocals is larger)
— mixing conventions silently biases $\sigma^2$ and hence the credibility assigned to backtests.

## The dispersion ledger (variance flow — the logic of every later step)

> **Note on logic.** The estimator's whole job is an accounting exercise: split every observable dispersion into
> *information* and *noise*, keep the former, discard the latter, and price what remains. This section is that ledger;
> each later formula moves exactly one entry of it.

Start from the OOS side, where the model is clean ($\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}} = S + \nu$, independent parts):

$$
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}_{V\ \text{: observed OOS spread}}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(S\big)}_{\tau^2\ \text{: true spread}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}}_{\text{measurement noise}}
$$

- $V$ — how differently your strategies *appear* to have performed live. Directly computable.
- $\tau^2$ — how differently they *truly* perform. Not observable per strategy, but its total is:
  $\tau^2 = V - \sum_k \tilde w_k / T_k$ — the observed spread minus what finite yardsticks alone would generate.
- The noise term carries no information about anything; it exists because Sharpes are measured over finite windows.

The true spread then splits by what the backtest can and cannot see:

$$
\underbrace{\tau^2}_{\text{true spread}}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}_{\text{explained by IS (via the line)}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\sigma^2}_{\text{invisible to IS}}
$$

- The first piece is the dispersion of the fitted values $\alpha + \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ — the portion of genuine
  quality differences the backtest successfully predicts.
- $\sigma^2$ — the portion it cannot: how much true Sharpes still differ **among strategies with identical backtests**.
  In model terms, $\sigma^2$ collects everything the IS equation's noise ($\eta_k + \xi_k$) and compressed loading hide
  about truth. This is the method's central quantity; the pipeline exists to isolate it.

Stacking both, the observed OOS spread has exactly three sources:

$$
V \;=\; \underbrace{\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}_{\text{IS-explained truth}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sigma^2}_{\text{IS-invisible truth}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}}_{\text{measurement noise}},
\qquad
R^2 = \frac{\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}{V}.
$$

$$
V \;=\;  \underbrace{R^2 V}_{\text{IS-explained truth}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sigma^2}_{\text{IS-invisible truth}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}}_{\text{measurement noise}},
$$

This shows why raw $R^2$ *understates* the backtest: its denominator contains measurement noise that nothing could ever
explain. The honest question is not "what fraction of $V$ does IS explain" but "what fraction of $\tau^2$" — and the
pipeline answers it by removing the noise term before pricing anything.

**Information flow, in words:** of the observed OOS spread, first strip the yardstick noise; of what remains — genuine
quality differences — the line captures $\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}})$ and misses $\sigma^2$. The
missed part is exactly what only live trading can reveal, which is why $1/\sigma^2$ becomes the exchange rate between
backtest evidence and live years.

## The slope as attenuation coefficient

> **Note on logic.** The slope has two readings. The *definitional* one is what the pipeline computes and needs no
> model. The *measurement-model* one is where the intuition lives. Keep both, and know which is which.

**Definitional (model-free, the one the pipeline uses):**

$$
\beta \;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}
\;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ S\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)},
$$

the second equality because OOS noise $\nu$ is uncorrelated with the backtest — **the slope automatically targets
truth**, not the noisy OOS print. It is an *attenuation coefficient*: the fraction of an IS deviation that survives
into expected truth. A backtest one Sharpe unit above the book implies a true Sharpe only $\beta$ units above.

**Measurement-model reading (intuition):** plug the scaled IS equation in:

$$
\beta \;=\; \frac{a\,\tau^2}{a^2\tau^2 + \operatorname{Var}(\eta + \xi)},
\qquad a = 1 + \gamma .
$$

In the special case of unit loading ($\gamma = 0$, constant bias) this reduces to the classical

$$
\beta = \frac{\tau^2}{\tau^2 + \omega^2}
= \frac{\text{signal dispersion}}{\text{signal dispersion} + \text{noise dispersion}} \in (0,1)
$$

— slope = signal share of the backtest's cross-sectional variance, and $\beta = 0.4$ reads as "60% of the spread in
your backtests carries no forward information; regression to the mean by that fraction is arithmetic, not pessimism."
With selection ($\gamma < 0$) the general formula applies; the attenuation intuition survives — every independent
noise or distortion layer flattens $\beta$ further — but the clean "signal fraction" arithmetic is the unit-loading
special case, not an estimator. The pipeline is indifferent: it computes $\operatorname{Cov}_w/\operatorname{Var}_w$,
which is correct under any loading.

Attenuation composes: more parameters tried per strategy, more path selection, shorter IS windows — each shrinks
$\beta$. This makes $\beta$ the single most informative diagnostic of a research process: the fraction of backtest
differentiation that survives contact with reality, measured on your own book.

## Pass 1 — pilot weighted regression

> **Note on logic.** The efficient weight for each observation is the inverse of its total residual variance
> $\sigma^2 + 1/T_k$ — but $\sigma^2$ is only measurable *after* a fit. Pass 1 breaks the circle: weight by the part of
> the variance known a priori ($1/T_k$, i.e. $w \propto T_k$), fit, and extract a first $\sigma^2$. Misweighting costs
> efficiency, never consistency, so the pilot's $\sigma^2$ is valid, just noisy.

Weights $\tilde w_k = T_k \big/ \sum_j T_j$. Fit $\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k = \alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k + \varepsilon_k$:

$$
\beta = \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)},
\qquad
\alpha = \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w - \beta\,\bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w,
\qquad
\hat\varepsilon_k = \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - \alpha - \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k .
$$

Extract $\sigma^2$ by the ledger. Per strategy, the residual has two independent parts,

$$
\hat\varepsilon_k = \underbrace{\big(S_k - \alpha - \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k\big)}_{\text{truth around the line, var } \sigma^2}
+ \underbrace{\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - S_k\big)}_{\nu_k,\ \text{var } 1/T_k},
$$

so the measured residual variance is (IS-invisible truth) + (measurement noise), and the known part is subtracted:

$$
\sigma^2 \;=\; \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat\varepsilon_k^{\,2}}_{(1-R^2)V\ =\ \sigma^2\ +\ \sum_k \tilde w_k/T_k}
\;-\; \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}
\qquad (\text{truncate at a small positive floor}).
$$

Skipping the subtraction would blame the yardstick's shakiness on the backtest — concluding "IS is uninformative"
merely because the OOS windows are short. (If the subtraction hits the floor: residuals are fully explained by
measurement noise, the line is essentially exact, and $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$ is effectively unbounded — a
finding, not a failure.)

## Pass 2 — refit with consistent weights (iterate to fixed point)

$$
w_k = \frac{1}{\sigma^2 + \dfrac{1}{T_k}}
$$

Repeat the regression and the $\sigma^2$ extraction with the normalized $w_k$ replacing $\tilde w_k$ **everywhere** —
means, slope, residual variance, and the noise subtraction $\sum_k \tilde w_k / T_k$ all recomputed under the new
weights. One or two iterations converge. Final outputs: $\alpha,\ \beta,\ \sigma^2$, and

$$
T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} \;=\; \frac{1}{\sigma^2}
\qquad \text{[years of live evidence the debiased backtest is worth]} .
$$

> **Note on logic.** The pass-2 weight saturates at $1/\sigma^2$ for long windows: beyond
> $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$ live years, a strategy's residual is dominated by genuine dispersion, which more
> calendar time does not shrink — so extra years stop earning extra influence on the line. The pilot ($w \propto T_k$)
> over-weights long-lived strategies; pass 2 caps them. Only at the fixed point are the regression weights, the
> noise subtraction, and the Bayesian combination below all the same object $(\sigma^2 + 1/T_k)^{-1}$ — which is
> exactly the condition under which the mean-preservation identity holds.

## Forward estimate

Year-weighted average of the debiased backtest claim and the live record:

$$
\boxed{\;
\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k
= \frac{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}\,\big(\alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k\big) \;+\; T_k\,\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k}
{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}
\;}
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}\big(\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k\big) = \frac{1}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}
$$

Interpretation: the backtest contributes $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$ synthetic years centered on its debiased value
$\alpha + \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$; the live record contributes $T_k$ actual years centered on
$\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$; the estimate is the time-weighted average and its SE is $1/\sqrt{\text{total years}}$.
Calendar time appears only through information content: evidence is counted in years, and the live record overtakes
the backtest the day it has run longer than the backtest is worth.

## Deviation form (equivalent — the interpretable version)

Substituting $\alpha = \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w - \beta\,\bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w$ and regrouping around the book anchor
(the bars are the **pass-2 weighted means** — the fitted line passes through them, which is what makes the anchors
cancel exactly):

$$
\boxed{\;
\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w
\;=\;
\underbrace{\frac{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}\,\beta}_{b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k}
\left(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w\right)
\;+\;
\underbrace{\frac{T_k}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}}_{b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k}
\left(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w\right)
\;}
$$

Forward estimate = **book average + two shrunk deviations**. The two shrinkages mirror the measurement model exactly:

- **IS deviation shrunk twice.** Once by $\beta$ — the attenuation coefficient, discarding the dispersion contributed
  by $b_k$'s tilt and by IS noise rather than by truth — and once by the year-ratio, discounting the *predicted* value
  for the residual uncertainty $\sigma^2$ it still carries.
- **OOS deviation shrunk once.** Only by the year-ratio: OOS is unbiased ($b_k$ never enters its equation), so it
  needs no attenuation — its sole defect is measurement noise $1/T_k$, priced by exactly that weight. Its shrinkage
  toward the mean is real; it lives in the weight, not in a fitted intercept.
- **Conservatism gauge:** $b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k + b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k = 1 - (1-\beta)\,\frac{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}+T_k} < 1$;
  the shortfall from one is the total pull toward the mean for strategy $k$.
- **Mean preservation by inspection:** both deviation terms are $\tilde w$-mean-zero, so
  $\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k = \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w$ immediately.
- **Attribution:** *book base* + *backtest credit* $b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w)$
  + *live credit* $b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w)$. As a strategy ages,
  $b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \uparrow 1$ and $b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \downarrow 0$ at rates set entirely by
  $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$.
- **Testable:** regress realized future deviations on the pair of observed deviations; fitted coefficients should
  match $\big(b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k, b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k\big)$ at the relevant $T_k$.

## Dispersion accounting of the estimator itself

> **Note on logic.** A calibrated estimator must carry exactly the cross-sectional spread of the posterior means —
> no more (noise masquerading as differentiation), no less (real quality differences erased). Check the books:

$$
\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\text{fwd}}\big)
= \underbrace{\beta^2\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}_{\text{IS-explained truth, kept in full}}
+ \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\;\sigma^2\,\frac{T_k\,\sigma^2}{1+T_k\,\sigma^2}}_{\text{IS-invisible truth, kept in proportion to live evidence}},
$$

matching $\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\mathbb{E}[S_k \mid \text{data}]\big)$ term by term. Of the ledger's three entries:
IS-explained truth is kept in full; IS-invisible truth is admitted only to the degree each strategy's live record has
resolved it (the factor $T_k\sigma^2/(1+T_k\sigma^2)$, a weighted average since it varies with $T_k$); measurement
noise is kept not at all. Shrinking further would under-disperse — systematically underrating genuinely good
strategies; shrinking less would let noise pass as differentiation. This is the precise sense in which **no additional
regularization belongs**: the dispersion books balance.

## Built-in properties

- **All shrinkage included, once.** Pull toward the book mean lives inside $(\alpha,\beta)$; pull of noisy OOS toward
  the prediction lives inside the year-weights. Do **not** shrink again.
- **Mean preservation (exact at the pass-2 fixed point):**
  $\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$ under the pass-2 weights —
  the estimator redistributes credibility across strategies, never inflates the book.
- **Limits.** $T_k \to 0$: pure debiased backtest. $T_k \to \infty$: pure live record.
  Crossover at $T_k = T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$.

## Bayesian reading (why this is the whole method)

The regression estimates the conditional distribution of truth given the backtest:

$$
S_k \mid \hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}\big(\alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k,\ \sigma^2\big)
$$

— the regression **is** the prior, absorbing $\bar b$ into $\alpha$, the loading $1+\gamma$ into $\beta$, and
$\operatorname{Var}(\eta+\xi)$ into $\sigma^2$, without modeling any of them. The live record is the likelihood,
$\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \mid S_k \sim \mathcal{N}(S_k,\ 1/T_k)$ — unbiased because $b_k$ never enters the OOS
equation. The boxed formula is the conjugate posterior mean; the year-weights are the precisions.

## Extensions (same architecture, richer regression)

- **Multivariate IS:** replace $\alpha + \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ with $\alpha + \boldsymbol{\beta}^\top x_k$ for
  any IS feature vector (median- and mean-Sharpe together, IS skew, $\ln n_k$ for search intensity). Predictable parts
  of $\xi_k$ migrate from noise to signal; $\sigma^2$, $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$, the combination, the deviation
  form all carry over verbatim. Guard with a degrees-of-freedom correction in $\sigma^2$ and out-of-fold selection of
  features — an overfit credibility-map understates $\sigma^2$ and over-credits backtests.
- **Group structure:** per-family $(\alpha_f, \beta_f, \sigma^2_f)$ when overfitting severity differs by strategy type
  (50+ strategies per family).

## Reference implementation (skeleton)

```python
import numpy as np

def forward_sharpe(S_is, S_oos, T, n_iter=4, floor=1e-3):
    def wls(x, y, w):
        wn = w / w.sum()                            # normalized weights
        xb, yb = wn @ x, wn @ y                     # weighted means
        beta  = (wn @ ((x - xb) * (y - yb))) / (wn @ (x - xb) ** 2)
        alpha = yb - beta * xb
        eps   = y - alpha - beta * x
        return alpha, beta, wn @ eps**2, wn, xb, yb

    w = T.copy()                                    # pass 1: pilot weights ~ T_k
    for _ in range(n_iter):
        alpha, beta, res_var, wn, S_is_bar, S_oos_bar = wls(S_is, S_oos, w)
        sigma2 = max(res_var - wn @ (1.0 / T), floor)   # weighted noise subtraction
        w = 1.0 / (sigma2 + 1.0 / T)                # pass 2+: consistent weights

    T_eff = 1.0 / sigma2
    b_is  = T_eff / (T_eff + T) * beta              # deviation loadings
    b_oos = T / (T_eff + T)
    S_fwd = S_oos_bar + b_is * (S_is - S_is_bar) + b_oos * (S_oos - S_oos_bar)
    se    = 1.0 / np.sqrt(T_eff + T)
    return S_fwd, se, dict(alpha=alpha, beta=beta, sigma2=sigma2, T_eff=T_eff,
                           b_is=b_is, b_oos=b_oos,
                           S_is_bar=S_is_bar, S_oos_bar=S_oos_bar)
```











# Forward-Looking Sharpe Estimation via Two-Pass Weighted Regression

## Inputs

For each strategy $k = 1, \dots, K$:

| Symbol | Definition |
|---|---|
| $\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ | $\sqrt{252}\cdot\operatorname{median}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{IS}}_k)\,/\,\operatorname{std}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{IS}}_k)$ — annualized in-sample Sharpe. Median (not mean) because selection on favorable paths creates spurious positive skew in IS pnl; the median's bounded influence immunizes against it. |
| $\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$ | $\sqrt{252}\cdot\operatorname{mean}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k)\,/\,\operatorname{std}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k)$ — annualized out-of-sample Sharpe. |
| $T_k$ | Length of strategy $k$'s OOS window, **in years**. |

All dispersions below are **cross-sectional** — spread *across the $K$ strategies*, not time-series variance of any one
strategy's pnl.

## Truth first

The primary object is the **true Sharpe** $S_k$ — a property of the strategy itself, existing independently of any
measurement. Its cross-sectional dispersion,

$$
\tau^2 \;\equiv\; \operatorname{Var}_w\big(S\big),
$$

is how much your strategies genuinely differ in quality. Truth does not need IS or OOS; **IS and OOS are two lenses
pointed at it**, each adding its own distortions. The method's logic is therefore: (i) write down what each lens does
to truth; (ii) decompose each *measured* dispersion into its truth content and its distortion content; (iii) recover
as much of $\tau^2$ as the lenses jointly allow, and price honestly what they cannot recover.

## The measurement model — what each lens does

$$
\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \;=\; S_k \;+\; b_k \;+\; \eta_k,
\qquad\qquad
\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \;=\; S_k \;+\; \nu_k,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\nu_k) = \frac{1}{T_k}.
$$

- $\eta_k$ — IS sampling noise (finite backtest window, pnl randomness).
- $\nu_k$ — OOS sampling noise, with **known** variance $1/T_k$: the live window's length is the yardstick.
- $b_k$ — the overfitting/selection bias, and it is **strategy-dependent**: it grows with the number of variants
  tried, the luck of the selected path, the parameter count, the aggressiveness of median compression for that pnl
  shape. A constant bias was never the honest model.
- Crucially, $b_k$ appears **only in the IS equation.** The live period never passed through the selection machine,
  so the OOS lens is clean: $\mathbb{E}[\nu_k \mid S_k] = 0$. This single asymmetry drives the entire design.

### Decomposing the bias

Any cross-sectional random quantity splits into a mean, a truth-correlated part, and an orthogonal remainder:

$$
b_k \;=\; \underbrace{\bar b}_{\text{average bias}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\gamma\,\big(S_k - \bar S\big)}_{\text{truth-correlated part}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\xi_k}_{\text{idiosyncratic part},\ \perp S}.
$$

Substituting back, the IS lens is a **scaled** measurement:

$$
\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k
= \big(\bar b - \gamma\,\bar S\big)
\;+\; a\,S_k
\;+\; \underbrace{\eta_k + \xi_k}_{\text{total IS noise}},
\qquad a = 1+\gamma .
$$

### The loading $a$ — how strongly the IS lens responds to truth

$$
\boxed{\;
a \;\equiv\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\, S\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(S\big)}
\;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\, S\big)}{\tau^2}
\;}
$$

In words: *if a strategy's true Sharpe is one unit higher, how much higher does its IS statistic sit, on average?*
It is the projection of the lens onto truth — the mirror of $\beta$, which projects truth onto the lens.

- $a = 1$: constant bias only (classical errors-in-variables). Truth passes through at full scale.
- $a < 1$: the lens compresses. Two mechanisms push this way: **selection** makes $\gamma < 0$ — strategies that
  survived selection despite weak truth are exactly those whose bias was large (they needed the luck), while
  strong-truth strategies survived on modest luck, so $\operatorname{Cov}(b_k, S_k) < 0$: at the survival margin,
  bias substitutes for quality. And **median compression** — the median statistic responds sub-proportionally to
  shifts in the pnl mean.

**Identification.** $a$ contains the unobservable $S$, yet it is recoverable from fitted quantities. Because OOS noise
is orthogonal to the backtest, $\operatorname{Cov}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}, \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}) =
\operatorname{Cov}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}, S)$, hence

$$
a \;=\; \frac{\beta\,\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}{\tau^2},
\qquad
\tau^2 = V - \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}
\qquad\text{(all measurable)}.
$$

**The product identity.** The two projections multiply to the squared IS–truth correlation:

$$
a\,\beta \;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w^2\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}, S\big)}{\tau^2\,\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}
\;=\; \rho^2 .
$$

$a$ enters no estimator formula — the regression's $\beta$ re-inflates whatever compression $a$ imposes, automatically
(indeed $\beta > 1$ is possible when $a$ is small and noise is light). But $a$ is the diagnostic that measures how
strongly selection has distorted the backtest's *scale*, and it is what resolves the apparent paradox below.

### How the pipeline absorbs $b_k$ without ever modeling it

| Component of $b_k$ | Its effect on the IS lens | Absorbed by |
|---|---|---|
| $\bar b$ — average inflation | shifts the IS mean | $\alpha$ (intercept recenters onto the OOS anchor) |
| $\gamma\,(S_k-\bar S)$ — truth-correlated tilt | rescales the loading to $a = 1+\gamma$ | $\beta$ (slope adapts to whatever $a$ is) |
| $\xi_k$ — idiosyncratic scatter | extra noise on IS | flattens $\beta$ further and inflates $\sigma^2$ — its damage is *measured* and charged to the backtest's credibility |

Nothing about $b_k$ needs to be known, estimated, or corrected per strategy: its mean is killed by the intercept, its
systematic tilt by the slope, and its scatter is priced into $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$. This is the content of
"the regression is the prior."

## Weighted moments (used everywhere)

Let $\tilde w_k$ be the normalized regression weights ($\sum_k \tilde w_k = 1$; pilot: $\tilde w_k \propto T_k$,
final: $\tilde w_k \propto (\sigma^2 + 1/T_k)^{-1}$). For any $x_k, y_k$:

$$
\bar x_w = \sum_k \tilde w_k\, x_k,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}_w(x) = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,(x_k - \bar x_w)^2,
\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}_w(x, y) = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,(x_k - \bar x_w)(y_k - \bar y_w).
$$

Every variance, covariance, mean, $R^2$, and noise-average in this document uses **the same weights as the regression
pass it belongs to**. This is substantive, not cosmetic: the decompositions below are exact algebraic identities of
the fit only under one consistent weighting, and the weighted noise term $\sum_k \tilde w_k / T_k$ genuinely differs
from the unweighted mean of $1/T_k$ when windows are heterogeneous (Jensen: the unweighted average of reciprocals is
larger) — mixing conventions silently biases $\sigma^2$ and hence the credibility assigned to backtests.

## The dispersion ledger — decomposing what you measure

> **Note on logic.** You measure two dispersions, $\operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}})$ and
> $\operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}})$. Each is a lens reading: truth content plus that lens's distortions.
> The ledger decomposes both, entry by entry. Truth ($\tau^2$) is the target being read — it appears in both
> decompositions but is decomposed by neither: it exists by itself.

### The OOS lens reading

Independent parts, variances add:

$$
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}_{V\ \text{: measured}}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\tau^2}_{\text{truth content}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}}_{\text{yardstick noise}}
$$

Two entries only — the OOS lens is clean (no bias term), truth passes at loading one, and the noise is *known* from
the window lengths. This is what makes OOS the anchor of everything: it identifies $\tau^2$ by a single subtraction,

$$
\tau^2 = V - \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}.
$$

### The IS lens reading

Apply the scaled model and take variances term by term (the constant drops; $\eta, \xi \perp S$ by construction —
the truth-correlated part of the bias was already extracted into $a$):

$$
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}_{\text{measured}}
\;=\;
\underbrace{a^2\,\tau^2}_{\substack{\text{truth content,}\\ \text{transmitted through the loading}}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w(\eta)}_{\substack{\text{IS sampling}\\ \text{noise}}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)}_{\substack{\text{idiosyncratic}\\ \text{selection/overfit scatter}}}
$$

Three entries, each with its own economics:

- $a^2\tau^2$ — the signal content, and note it is truth *squared through the loading*: with $a < 1$ under selection,
  even the informative part of IS dispersion is smaller than $\tau^2$. This is why
  $\operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}) < \tau^2$ is possible — under constant bias it would be impossible
  (adding independent noise only widens a distribution), but with truth-anticorrelated bias it is the *expected
  signature* of selection, not a paradox.
- $\operatorname{Var}_w(\eta)$ — honest measurement noise; would exist with no selection at all; shrinks with IS
  window length.
- $\operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)$ — the **selection tax**: how much pure luck varies across selected strategies. Grows
  with search intensity; does **not** shrink with backtest length — a longer backtest of an overfit strategy is a
  longer overfit backtest.

**Partial separation of the entries (diagnostic).** From the fit: $a = \beta\operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}})/\tau^2$
pins down $a^2\tau^2$, hence the combined noise $\operatorname{Var}_w(\eta) + \operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)$ as a
remainder. Splitting further needs one instrument: the analytic or bootstrapped sampling variance of the median-Sharpe
over $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ estimates $\operatorname{Var}_w(\eta)$, leaving $\operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)$ — the selection
tax in Sharpe² — as a direct, book-level measurement of the research process. It is the one entry that better process
(fewer trials per launched strategy, preregistration, untouched holdouts) shrinks and nothing else does.

### The recovery equation — how much of $\tau^2$ the IS lens returns

The regression of OOS on IS recovers, in its fitted values, exactly the correlated part of truth:

$$
\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)
= \beta^2\,\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)
= \rho^2\,\tau^2
\qquad (\rho^2 = a\beta),
$$

and leaves unrecovered

$$
\sigma^2 \;\equiv\; \operatorname{Var}_w\big(S \mid \hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big) \;=\; \big(1-\rho^2\big)\,\tau^2
$$

— how much true Sharpes still differ **among strategies with identical backtests**. Read the loading-cancellation in
$\rho^2\tau^2 = a\beta\,\tau^2$: whether the lens transmits truth at scale $0.2$ or $1.0$ is irrelevant to how much of
$\tau^2$ is recovered — the regression re-inflates any compression automatically; only the *correlation* between lens
and truth matters. $\sigma^2$ is the method's central quantity: it is what only live trading can reveal, and
$1/\sigma^2$ becomes the exchange rate between backtest evidence and live years.

Stacking the OOS reading and the recovery equation gives the full accounting of the measured OOS spread:

$$
V \;=\; \underbrace{\rho^2\,\tau^2}_{\text{truth, recovered via IS}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sigma^2}_{\text{truth, invisible to IS}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}}_{\text{yardstick noise}},
\qquad
R^2 = \frac{\rho^2\,\tau^2}{V}.
$$

This shows why raw $R^2$ *understates* the backtest: its denominator contains yardstick noise that nothing could ever
explain. The honest question is not "what fraction of $V$ does IS explain" but "what fraction of $\tau^2$" — that is
$\rho^2$ — and the pipeline answers it by removing the noise term before pricing anything.

## The slope as attenuation coefficient

> **Note on logic.** The slope has two readings. The *definitional* one is what the pipeline computes and needs no
> model. The *measurement-model* one is where the intuition lives. Keep both, and know which is which.

**Definitional (model-free, the one the pipeline uses):**

$$
\beta \;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}
\;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ S\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)},
$$

the second equality because OOS noise $\nu$ is uncorrelated with the backtest — **the slope automatically targets
truth**, not the noisy OOS print. It is an *attenuation coefficient*: the fraction of an IS deviation that survives
into expected truth. A backtest one Sharpe unit above the book implies a true Sharpe only $\beta$ units above.

**Measurement-model reading (intuition):** plug the IS lens decomposition in:

$$
\beta \;=\; \frac{a\,\tau^2}{a^2\tau^2 + \operatorname{Var}_w(\eta) + \operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)}
\;=\; \frac{\text{truth transmitted}}{\text{truth transmitted} + \text{total lens noise}} \times \frac{1}{a}.
$$

In the unit-loading special case ($\gamma = 0$, constant bias) this reduces to the classical

$$
\beta = \frac{\tau^2}{\tau^2 + \omega^2}
= \frac{\text{signal dispersion}}{\text{signal dispersion} + \text{noise dispersion}} \in (0,1)
$$

— slope = signal share of the lens's variance, and $\beta = 0.4$ reads as "60% of the spread in your backtests carries
no forward information; regression to the mean by that fraction is arithmetic, not pessimism." With selection
($a < 1$) the general formula applies; the attenuation intuition survives — every independent noise or distortion
layer flattens $\beta$ further — but the clean "signal fraction" arithmetic is the special case, not an estimator.
The pipeline is indifferent: it computes $\operatorname{Cov}_w/\operatorname{Var}_w$, correct under any loading.

Attenuation composes: more parameters tried per strategy, more path selection, shorter IS windows — each shrinks
$\beta$. This makes $\beta$ the single most informative diagnostic of a research process: the fraction of backtest
differentiation that survives contact with reality, measured on your own book.

## Pass 1 — pilot weighted regression

> **Note on logic.** The efficient weight for each observation is the inverse of its total residual variance
> $\sigma^2 + 1/T_k$ — but $\sigma^2$ is only measurable *after* a fit. Pass 1 breaks the circle: weight by the part
> of the variance known a priori ($1/T_k$, i.e. $w \propto T_k$), fit, and extract a first $\sigma^2$. Misweighting
> costs efficiency, never consistency, so the pilot's $\sigma^2$ is valid, just noisy.

Weights $\tilde w_k = T_k \big/ \sum_j T_j$. Fit $\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k = \alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k + \varepsilon_k$:

$$
\beta = \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)},
\qquad
\alpha = \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w - \beta\,\bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w,
\qquad
\hat\varepsilon_k = \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - \alpha - \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k .
$$

Extract $\sigma^2$ by the ledger. Per strategy, the residual has two independent parts,

$$
\hat\varepsilon_k = \underbrace{\big(S_k - \alpha - \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k\big)}_{\text{truth around the line, var } \sigma^2}
+ \underbrace{\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - S_k\big)}_{\nu_k,\ \text{var } 1/T_k},
$$

so the measured residual variance is (IS-invisible truth) + (yardstick noise), and the known part is subtracted:

$$
\sigma^2 \;=\; \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat\varepsilon_k^{\,2}}_{(1-R^2)V\ =\ \sigma^2\ +\ \sum_k \tilde w_k/T_k}
\;-\; \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}
\qquad (\text{truncate at a small positive floor}).
$$

Skipping the subtraction would blame the yardstick's shakiness on the backtest — concluding "IS is uninformative"
merely because the OOS windows are short. (If the subtraction hits the floor: residuals are fully explained by
measurement noise, the line is essentially exact, and $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$ is effectively unbounded — a
finding, not a failure.)

## Pass 2 — refit with consistent weights (iterate to fixed point)

$$
w_k = \frac{1}{\sigma^2 + \dfrac{1}{T_k}}
$$

Repeat the regression and the $\sigma^2$ extraction with the normalized $w_k$ replacing $\tilde w_k$ **everywhere** —
means, slope, residual variance, and the noise subtraction $\sum_k \tilde w_k / T_k$ all recomputed under the new
weights. One or two iterations converge. Final outputs: $\alpha,\ \beta,\ \sigma^2$, and

$$
T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} \;=\; \frac{1}{\sigma^2}
\qquad \text{[years of live evidence the debiased backtest is worth]} .
$$

> **Note on logic.** The pass-2 weight saturates at $1/\sigma^2$ for long windows: beyond
> $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$ live years, a strategy's residual is dominated by genuine dispersion, which more
> calendar time does not shrink — so extra years stop earning extra influence on the line. The pilot
> ($w \propto T_k$) over-weights long-lived strategies; pass 2 caps them. Only at the fixed point are the regression
> weights, the noise subtraction, and the Bayesian combination below all the same object $(\sigma^2 + 1/T_k)^{-1}$ —
> which is exactly the condition under which the mean-preservation identity holds.

## Forward estimate

Year-weighted average of the debiased backtest claim and the live record:

$$
\boxed{\;
\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k
= \frac{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}\,\big(\alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k\big) \;+\; T_k\,\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k}
{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}
\;}
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}\big(\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k\big) = \frac{1}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}
$$

Interpretation: the backtest contributes $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$ synthetic years centered on its debiased value
$\alpha + \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$; the live record contributes $T_k$ actual years centered on
$\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$; the estimate is the time-weighted average and its SE is $1/\sqrt{\text{total years}}$.
Evidence is counted in years, and the live record overtakes the backtest the day it has run longer than the backtest
is worth.

## Deviation form (equivalent — the interpretable version)

Substituting $\alpha = \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w - \beta\,\bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w$ and regrouping around the book anchor
(the bars are the **pass-2 weighted means** — the fitted line passes through them, which is what makes the anchors
cancel exactly):

$$
\boxed{\;
\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w
\;=\;
\underbrace{\frac{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}\,\beta}_{b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k}
\left(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w\right)
\;+\;
\underbrace{\frac{T_k}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}}_{b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k}
\left(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w\right)
\;}
$$

Forward estimate = **book average + two shrunk deviations**. The two shrinkages mirror the measurement model exactly:

- **IS deviation shrunk twice.** Once by $\beta$ — the attenuation coefficient, discarding the dispersion contributed
  by $b_k$'s tilt and by IS noise rather than by truth — and once by the year-ratio, discounting the *predicted*
  value for the residual uncertainty $\sigma^2$ it still carries.
- **OOS deviation shrunk once.** Only by the year-ratio: the OOS lens is unbiased ($b_k$ never enters its equation),
  so it needs no attenuation — its sole defect is yardstick noise $1/T_k$, priced by exactly that weight. Its
  shrinkage toward the mean is real; it lives in the weight, not in a fitted intercept.
- **Conservatism gauge:** $b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k + b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k = 1 - (1-\beta)\,\frac{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}+T_k} < 1$;
  the shortfall from one is the total pull toward the mean for strategy $k$.
- **Mean preservation by inspection:** both deviation terms are $\tilde w$-mean-zero, so
  $\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k = \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w$ immediately.
- **Attribution:** *book base* + *backtest credit* $b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w)$
  + *live credit* $b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w)$. As a strategy ages,
  $b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \uparrow 1$ and $b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \downarrow 0$ at rates set entirely by
  $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$.
- **Testable:** regress realized future deviations on the pair of observed deviations; fitted coefficients should
  match $\big(b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k, b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k\big)$ at the relevant $T_k$.

## Dispersion accounting of the estimator itself

> **Note on logic.** A calibrated estimator must carry exactly the cross-sectional spread of the posterior means —
> no more (noise masquerading as differentiation), no less (real quality differences erased). Check the books:

$$
\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\text{fwd}}\big)
= \underbrace{\rho^2\,\tau^2}_{\text{truth recovered via IS, kept in full}}
+ \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\;\sigma^2\,\frac{T_k\,\sigma^2}{1+T_k\,\sigma^2}}_{\text{IS-invisible truth, kept in proportion to live evidence}},
$$

matching $\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\mathbb{E}[S_k \mid \text{data}]\big)$ term by term. Of the ledger's entries: truth
recovered via IS is kept in full; IS-invisible truth is admitted only to the degree each strategy's live record has
resolved it (the factor $T_k\sigma^2/(1+T_k\sigma^2)$, a weighted average since it varies with $T_k$); yardstick noise
is kept not at all. Shrinking further would under-disperse — systematically underrating genuinely good strategies;
shrinking less would let noise pass as differentiation. This is the precise sense in which **no additional
regularization belongs**: the dispersion books balance.

## Built-in properties

- **All shrinkage included, once.** Pull toward the book mean lives inside $(\alpha,\beta)$; pull of noisy OOS toward
  the prediction lives inside the year-weights. Do **not** shrink again.
- **Mean preservation (exact at the pass-2 fixed point):**
  $\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$ under the pass-2 weights —
  the estimator redistributes credibility across strategies, never inflates the book.
- **Limits.** $T_k \to 0$: pure debiased backtest. $T_k \to \infty$: pure live record.
  Crossover at $T_k = T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$.

## Bayesian reading (why this is the whole method)

The regression estimates the conditional distribution of truth given the backtest:

$$
S_k \mid \hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}\big(\alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k,\ \sigma^2\big)
$$

— the regression **is** the prior, absorbing $\bar b$ into $\alpha$, the loading $a = 1+\gamma$ into $\beta$, and
$\operatorname{Var}_w(\eta+\xi)$ into $\sigma^2$, without modeling any of them. The live record is the likelihood,
$\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \mid S_k \sim \mathcal{N}(S_k,\ 1/T_k)$ — unbiased because $b_k$ never enters the OOS
equation. The boxed formula is the conjugate posterior mean; the year-weights are the precisions.

## Extensions (same architecture, richer regression)

- **Multivariate IS:** replace $\alpha + \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ with $\alpha + \boldsymbol{\beta}^\top x_k$ for
  any IS feature vector (median- and mean-Sharpe together, IS skew, $\ln n_k$ for search intensity). Predictable
  parts of $\xi_k$ migrate from noise to signal; $\sigma^2$, $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$, the combination, the
  deviation form all carry over verbatim. Guard with a degrees-of-freedom correction in $\sigma^2$ and out-of-fold
  selection of features — an overfit credibility-map understates $\sigma^2$ and over-credits backtests.
- **Group structure:** per-family $(\alpha_f, \beta_f, \sigma^2_f)$ when overfitting severity differs by strategy
  type (50+ strategies per family).

## Reference implementation (skeleton)

```python
import numpy as np

def forward_sharpe(S_is, S_oos, T, n_iter=4, floor=1e-3):
    def wls(x, y, w):
        wn = w / w.sum()                            # normalized weights
        xb, yb = wn @ x, wn @ y                     # weighted means
        beta  = (wn @ ((x - xb) * (y - yb))) / (wn @ (x - xb) ** 2)
        alpha = yb - beta * xb
        eps   = y - alpha - beta * x
        return alpha, beta, wn @ eps**2, wn, xb, yb

    w = T.copy()                                    # pass 1: pilot weights ~ T_k
    for _ in range(n_iter):
        alpha, beta, res_var, wn, S_is_bar, S_oos_bar = wls(S_is, S_oos, w)
        sigma2 = max(res_var - wn @ (1.0 / T), floor)   # weighted noise subtraction
        w = 1.0 / (sigma2 + 1.0 / T)                # pass 2+: consistent weights

    T_eff = 1.0 / sigma2
    b_is  = T_eff / (T_eff + T) * beta              # deviation loadings
    b_oos = T / (T_eff + T)
    S_fwd = S_oos_bar + b_is * (S_is - S_is_bar) + b_oos * (S_oos - S_oos_bar)
    se    = 1.0 / np.sqrt(T_eff + T)

    # Diagnostics: truth dispersion, loading, IS-truth correlation
    V     = None  # set below from the final pass for clarity
    wn_f  = w / w.sum()
    yb    = wn_f @ S_oos
    V     = wn_f @ (S_oos - yb) ** 2
    tau2  = max(V - wn_f @ (1.0 / T), floor)
    var_is = wn_f @ (S_is - wn_f @ S_is) ** 2
    a     = beta * var_is / tau2                    # loading of IS on truth
    rho2  = a * beta                                # squared IS-truth correlation

    return S_fwd, se, dict(alpha=alpha, beta=beta, sigma2=sigma2, T_eff=T_eff,
                           b_is=b_is, b_oos=b_oos, tau2=tau2, a=a, rho2=rho2,
                           S_is_bar=S_is_bar, S_oos_bar=S_oos_bar)
```




# Forward-Looking Sharpe Estimation via Two-Pass Weighted Regression

## Inputs

For each strategy $k = 1, \dots, K$:

| Symbol | Definition |
|---|---|
| $\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ | $\sqrt{252}\cdot\operatorname{median}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{IS}}_k)\,/\,\operatorname{std}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{IS}}_k)$ — annualized in-sample Sharpe. Median (not mean) because selection on favorable paths creates spurious positive skew in IS pnl; the median's bounded influence immunizes against it. |
| $\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$ | $\sqrt{252}\cdot\operatorname{mean}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k)\,/\,\operatorname{std}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k)$ — annualized out-of-sample Sharpe. |
| $T_k$ | Length of strategy $k$'s OOS window, **in years**. |

All dispersions below are **cross-sectional** — spread *across the $K$ strategies*, not time-series variance of any
one strategy's pnl.

## Truth first

The primary object is the **true Sharpe** $S_k$ — a property of the strategy itself, existing independently of any
measurement. Its cross-sectional dispersion,

$$
\tau^2 \;\equiv\; \operatorname{Var}_w\big(S\big),
$$

is how much your strategies genuinely differ in quality. Truth does not need IS or OOS; **IS and OOS are two lenses
pointed at it**, each adding its own distortions. The method: write down what each lens does to truth; decompose each
*measured* dispersion into truth content and distortion content; recover as much of $\tau^2$ as the lenses jointly
allow, and price honestly what they cannot recover.

## The measurement model — what each lens does

$$
\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \;=\; S_k \;+\; b_k \;+\; \eta_k,
\qquad\qquad
\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \;=\; S_k \;+\; \nu_k,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\nu_k) = \frac{1}{T_k}.
$$

- $\eta_k$ — IS sampling noise (finite backtest window, pnl randomness).
- $\nu_k$ — OOS sampling noise, with **known** variance $1/T_k$: the live window's length is the yardstick.
- $b_k$ — the overfitting/selection bias, **strategy-dependent**: it grows with the number of variants tried, the
  luck of the selected path, the parameter count, the aggressiveness of median compression for that pnl shape.
- Crucially, $b_k$ appears **only in the IS equation.** The live period never passed through the selection machine,
  so the OOS lens is clean: $\mathbb{E}[\nu_k \mid S_k] = 0$. This single asymmetry drives the entire design.

### Decomposing the bias

Any cross-sectional random quantity splits into a mean, a truth-correlated part, and an orthogonal remainder:

$$
b_k \;=\; \underbrace{\bar b}_{\text{average bias}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\gamma\,\big(S_k - \bar S\big)}_{\text{truth-correlated part}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\xi_k}_{\text{idiosyncratic part},\ \perp S}.
$$

Substituting back, the IS lens is a **scaled** measurement:

$$
\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k
= \big(\bar b - \gamma\,\bar S\big)
\;+\; a\,S_k
\;+\; \underbrace{\eta_k + \xi_k}_{\text{total IS noise}},
\qquad a = 1+\gamma .
$$

### The loading $a$ — how strongly the IS lens responds to truth

$$
\boxed{\;
a \;\equiv\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\, S\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(S\big)}
\;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\, S\big)}{\tau^2}
\;}
$$

In words: *if a strategy's true Sharpe is one unit higher, how much higher does its IS statistic sit, on average?*
It is the projection of the lens onto truth — the mirror of $\beta$, which projects truth onto the lens.

- $a = 1$: constant bias only (classical errors-in-variables). Truth passes through at full scale.
- $a < 1$: the lens compresses. Two mechanisms: **selection** makes $\gamma < 0$ — strategies that survived selection
  despite weak truth are exactly those whose bias was large (they needed the luck), while strong-truth strategies
  survived on modest luck, so $\operatorname{Cov}(b_k, S_k) < 0$: at the survival margin, bias substitutes for
  quality. And **median compression** — the median statistic responds sub-proportionally to shifts in the pnl mean.

**Identification.** $a$ contains the unobservable $S$, yet is recoverable: because OOS noise is orthogonal to the
backtest, $\operatorname{Cov}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}, \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}) = \operatorname{Cov}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}, S)$, hence

$$
a \;=\; \frac{\beta\,\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}{\tau^2},
\qquad\text{with}\qquad
\tau^2 = V - \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}
\qquad\text{(all measurable)},
$$

and the two projections multiply to the squared IS–truth correlation:

$$
a\,\beta \;=\; \rho^2 .
$$

$a$ enters no estimator formula — the regression's $\beta$ re-inflates whatever compression $a$ imposes,
automatically. But it is the diagnostic that measures how strongly selection has distorted the backtest's *scale*.

### How the pipeline absorbs $b_k$ without ever modeling it

| Component of $b_k$ | Its effect on the IS lens | Absorbed by |
|---|---|---|
| $\bar b$ — average inflation | shifts the IS mean | $\alpha$ (intercept recenters onto the OOS anchor) |
| $\gamma\,(S_k-\bar S)$ — truth-correlated tilt | rescales the loading to $a = 1+\gamma$ | $\beta$ (slope adapts to whatever $a$ is) |
| $\xi_k$ — idiosyncratic scatter | extra noise on IS | flattens $\beta$ further and inflates $\sigma^2$ — its damage is *measured* and charged to the backtest's credibility |

Nothing about $b_k$ needs to be known, estimated, or corrected per strategy: mean killed by the intercept, tilt by the
slope, scatter priced into $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$. This is the content of "the regression is the prior."

## Weighted moments (used everywhere)

Let $\tilde w_k$ be the normalized regression weights ($\sum_k \tilde w_k = 1$; pilot: $\tilde w_k \propto T_k$,
final: $\tilde w_k \propto (\sigma^2 + 1/T_k)^{-1}$). For any $x_k, y_k$:

$$
\bar x_w = \sum_k \tilde w_k\, x_k,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}_w(x) = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,(x_k - \bar x_w)^2,
\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}_w(x, y) = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,(x_k - \bar x_w)(y_k - \bar y_w).
$$

Every variance, covariance, mean, $R^2$, and noise-average uses **the same weights as the regression pass it belongs
to**. Substantive, not cosmetic: the decompositions below are exact identities of the fit only under one consistent
weighting, and $\sum_k \tilde w_k / T_k$ genuinely differs from the unweighted mean of $1/T_k$ when windows are
heterogeneous (Jensen) — mixing conventions silently biases $\sigma^2$.

## Three regressions, three variances — the core of the method

> **Note on logic.** There are three regressions in play: one you run, and two you want. The method is the statement
> that both of the ones you want are *computable* — one inherits its coefficients from the regression you run, the
> other's coefficients are known analytically. Everything else in this document is bookkeeping around this block.

### The three regressions

$$
\begin{array}{llll}
\textbf{(A) OOS on IS} & \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k = \alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k + \varepsilon_k
& \text{observable — the one you run} \\[4pt]
\textbf{(B) Truth on IS} & S_k = \alpha^\star + \beta^\star\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k + e_k
& \text{unobservable — the debiasing map} \\[4pt]
\textbf{(C) Truth on OOS} & S_k = \alpha^\dagger + \beta^\dagger\,\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k + e^\dagger_k
& \text{unobservable — the live-record shrinkage}
\end{array}
$$

**(B) inherits from (A).** Since $\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}} = S + \nu$ with $\nu \perp \hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}$:

$$
\beta = \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\, S + \nu)}{\operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}})} = \beta^\star,
\qquad
\alpha = \alpha^\star,
\qquad
\varepsilon_k = e_k + \nu_k .
$$

Noise in the dependent variable does not move a regression line: **(A) and (B) are the same line.** OOS noise
contaminates only the residual, which is why the coefficients are kept as-is and only the residual variance is
cleaned:

$$
\operatorname{Var}_w(\varepsilon) = \sigma^2 + \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\tfrac{1}{T_k}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\sigma^2 \equiv \operatorname{Var}_w(e) = \operatorname{Var}_w(\varepsilon) - \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\tfrac{1}{T_k}.
$$

**(C) is known analytically.** The OOS lens is the exact unit-loading model ($b_k$ never enters it), so the classical
attenuation formula holds *literally*, with known noise:

$$
\beta^\dagger_k = \frac{\tau^2}{\tau^2 + \tfrac{1}{T_k}} = \frac{T_k\,\tau^2}{1 + T_k\,\tau^2},
\qquad
\alpha^\dagger_k = \big(1-\beta^\dagger_k\big)\,\bar S_w,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}\big(e^\dagger_k\big) = \frac{\tau^2}{1 + T_k\,\tau^2}.
$$

No fit is run for (C): its coefficients are functions of two already-known numbers ($\tau^2$ and $T_k$). This is
exactly what "the OOS lens is clean" buys.

### The three variances

Each measured dispersion decomposes against truth, each via one model equation:

$$
\begin{array}{lll}
\textbf{OOS lens:} & V \equiv \operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big) = \tau^2 + \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\tfrac{1}{T_k}
& \text{from } \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}} = S + \nu \\[6pt]
\textbf{IS lens:} & \operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big) = a^2\tau^2 + \operatorname{Var}_w(\eta) + \operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)
& \text{from } \hat S^{\mathrm{IS}} = c + aS + \eta + \xi \\[6pt]
\textbf{Regression (A)=(B):} & \beta^2\,\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big) = \rho^2\,\tau^2,
\qquad \sigma^2 = \big(1-\rho^2\big)\,\tau^2
& \text{fitted-value and residual variance}
\end{array}
$$

Readings, entry by entry:

- **OOS lens: two entries.** Truth at loading one, plus known yardstick noise. This is what makes OOS the anchor: it
  identifies $\tau^2$ by a single subtraction, $\tau^2 = V - \sum_k \tilde w_k/T_k$.
- **IS lens: three entries.** Truth *squared through the loading* ($a<1$ under selection — which is why
  $\operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}) < \tau^2$ is possible: not a paradox but the signature of
  truth-anticorrelated bias); honest sampling noise $\operatorname{Var}_w(\eta)$ (shrinks with IS length); and the
  **selection tax** $\operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)$ — the scatter of pure luck across selected strategies, which grows
  with search intensity and does *not* shrink with backtest length.
- **Regression: the recovery.** The fitted values carry $\rho^2\tau^2$ of truth — and note the loading cancels
  ($\rho^2 = a\beta$): whether the lens transmits truth at scale $0.2$ or $1.0$ is irrelevant, the regression
  re-inflates any compression; only the *correlation* with truth matters. What is not recovered, $\sigma^2$, is how
  much true Sharpes still differ **among strategies with identical backtests** — the method's central quantity.

### The identification chain

$$
V \ \xrightarrow{\ \text{subtract known noise}\ }\ \tau^2
\ \xrightarrow{\ \text{with } \beta,\ \operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}})\ }\ a,\ \ \rho^2 = a\beta
\ \xrightarrow{\quad}\ \sigma^2 = (1-\rho^2)\,\tau^2
\ \xrightarrow{\quad}\ T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} = \tfrac{1}{\sigma^2}
$$

OOS identifies truth's total; the regression identifies truth's IS-visible share; the difference prices the backtest.
**Consistency check (free unit test):** the residual-based and share-based expressions for $\sigma^2$ —
$\operatorname{Var}_w(\varepsilon) - \sum_k \tilde w_k/T_k$ and $(1-\rho^2)\tau^2$ — are algebraically identical (both
equal $\tau^2 - \beta^2\operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}})$); compute both and assert equality.

### How the estimator composes (B) and (C)

Rewrite (C)'s prediction as shrinkage toward the mean:

$$
\mathbb{E}\big[S_k \mid \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k\big]
= \bar S_w + \frac{T_k\,\tau^2}{1 + T_k\,\tau^2}\,\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - \bar S_w\big),
$$

and compare with the deviation-form OOS loading of the final estimator, $b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k = \frac{T_k\,\sigma^2}{1 + T_k\,\sigma^2}$:
**same functional form, one substitution $\tau^2 \to \sigma^2$.** The final estimator is literally regression (B) for
the center and regression (C) for the update — with (C) operating on the dispersion *remaining after* IS conditioning,
$\sigma^2 = (1-\rho^2)\tau^2$, rather than on the full $\tau^2$. This settles the recurring question "should OOS be
regressed/shrunk like IS was": it **is**, via (C) — but (C)'s coefficients are known analytically, so no fit is run,
and the IS conditioning has already narrowed the prior, so running an *empirical* OOS-side regression on top would
re-estimate the known and double-apply the prior.

**Ablation diagnostic.** (C) alone — computable without any IS data — gives the pure-live-record estimator
$\hat S^{(C)}_k = \bar S_w + \frac{T_k\tau^2}{1+T_k\tau^2}\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - \bar S_w\big)$. The full
estimator should beat it out-of-sample by exactly the margin the IS channel claims ($\rho^2$ of variance). If it does
not, the IS channel's contribution is illusory — a one-line test of the entire backtest apparatus.

## The slope as attenuation coefficient

**Definitional (model-free, the one the pipeline uses):**

$$
\beta \;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}
\;=\; \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ S\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}
$$

— the slope automatically targets truth (second equality: $\nu \perp \hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}$). It is an *attenuation
coefficient*: the fraction of an IS deviation that survives into expected truth. A backtest one Sharpe unit above the
book implies a true Sharpe only $\beta$ units above.

**Measurement-model reading (intuition):** plug the IS lens decomposition in:

$$
\beta \;=\; \frac{a\,\tau^2}{a^2\tau^2 + \operatorname{Var}_w(\eta) + \operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)} .
$$

In the unit-loading special case ($a=1$) this is the classical
$\beta = \tau^2/(\tau^2+\omega^2) = \text{signal}/(\text{signal}+\text{noise}) \in (0,1)$: $\beta = 0.4$ reads as
"60% of the spread in your backtests carries no forward information; regression to the mean by that fraction is
arithmetic, not pessimism." With selection ($a<1$) the general formula applies; the attenuation intuition survives —
every independent noise or distortion layer flattens $\beta$ — but the clean signal-fraction arithmetic is the special
case, not an estimator. (Note the contrast: on the OOS side, regression (C), the unit-loading formula is *exact*.)

Attenuation composes: more parameters tried per strategy, more path selection, shorter IS windows — each shrinks
$\beta$. This makes $\beta$ the single most informative diagnostic of a research process: the fraction of backtest
differentiation that survives contact with reality, measured on your own book.

## Pass 1 — pilot weighted regression

> **Note on logic.** The efficient weight is the inverse of each observation's total residual variance
> $\sigma^2 + 1/T_k$ — but $\sigma^2$ is only measurable *after* a fit. Pass 1 breaks the circle: weight by the known
> part ($1/T_k$, i.e. $w \propto T_k$), fit, extract a first $\sigma^2$. Misweighting costs efficiency, never
> consistency, so the pilot's $\sigma^2$ is valid, just noisy.

Weights $\tilde w_k = T_k \big/ \sum_j T_j$. Fit regression (A):

$$
\beta = \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)},
\qquad
\alpha = \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w - \beta\,\bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w,
\qquad
\hat\varepsilon_k = \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - \alpha - \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k,
$$

$$
\sigma^2 \;=\; \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat\varepsilon_k^{\,2} \;-\; \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\frac{1}{T_k}
\qquad (\text{truncate at a small positive floor}).
$$

Skipping the subtraction would blame the yardstick's shakiness on the backtest — concluding "IS is uninformative"
merely because the OOS windows are short. (If the subtraction hits the floor: residuals are fully explained by
measurement noise, the line is essentially exact, and $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$ is effectively unbounded — a
finding, not a failure.)

## Pass 2 — refit with consistent weights (iterate to fixed point)

$$
w_k = \frac{1}{\sigma^2 + \dfrac{1}{T_k}}
$$

Repeat the regression and the $\sigma^2$ extraction with the normalized $w_k$ replacing $\tilde w_k$ **everywhere**.
One or two iterations converge. Final outputs: $\alpha,\ \beta,\ \sigma^2$, and

$$
T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} \;=\; \frac{1}{\sigma^2}
\qquad \text{[years of live evidence the debiased backtest is worth]} .
$$

> **Note on logic.** The pass-2 weight saturates at $1/\sigma^2$ for long windows: beyond
> $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$ live years a strategy's residual is dominated by genuine dispersion, which more
> calendar time does not shrink — extra years stop earning extra influence on the line. Only at the fixed point are
> the regression weights, the noise subtraction, and the Bayesian combination all the same object
> $(\sigma^2 + 1/T_k)^{-1}$ — the condition under which the mean-preservation identity holds.

## Forward estimate

Year-weighted average of the debiased backtest claim and the live record:

$$
\boxed{\;
\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k
= \frac{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}\,\big(\alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k\big) \;+\; T_k\,\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k}
{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}
\;}
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}\big(\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k\big) = \frac{1}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}
$$

The backtest contributes $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$ synthetic years centered on its debiased value (regression
(B)); the live record contributes $T_k$ actual years centered on $\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$; the estimate is the
time-weighted average, SE $= 1/\sqrt{\text{total years}}$. The live record overtakes the backtest the day it has run
longer than the backtest is worth.

## Deviation form (equivalent — the interpretable version)

Substituting $\alpha = \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w - \beta\,\bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w$ and regrouping around the book anchor
(bars are the **pass-2 weighted means** — the fitted line passes through them, which makes the anchors cancel exactly):

$$
\boxed{\;
\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w
\;=\;
\underbrace{\frac{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}\,\beta}_{b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k}
\left(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{IS}}_w\right)
\;+\;
\underbrace{\frac{T_k}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}}_{b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k}
\left(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k - \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w\right)
\;}
$$

Forward estimate = **book average + two shrunk deviations**:

- **IS deviation shrunk twice.** By $\beta$ (attenuation: discards the dispersion contributed by $b_k$'s tilt and IS
  noise) and by the year-ratio (discounts the prediction for the residual uncertainty $\sigma^2$ it still carries).
- **OOS deviation shrunk once.** Only by the year-ratio — this *is* regression (C) at dispersion $\sigma^2$: the OOS
  lens is unbiased, so it needs no attenuation; its sole defect is yardstick noise $1/T_k$, priced by exactly that
  weight. The shrinkage is real; it lives in the weight, not in a fitted intercept.
- **Conservatism gauge:** $b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k + b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k = 1 - (1-\beta)\,\frac{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}}{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}+T_k} < 1$;
  the shortfall from one is the total pull toward the mean.
- **Mean preservation by inspection:** both deviation terms are $\tilde w$-mean-zero, so
  $\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k = \bar S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_w$.
- **Attribution:** *book base* + *backtest credit* + *live credit*. As a strategy ages, $b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \uparrow 1$
  and $b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \downarrow 0$ at rates set entirely by $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$.
- **Testable:** regress realized future deviations on the pair of observed deviations; fitted coefficients should
  match $\big(b^{\mathrm{IS}}_k, b^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k\big)$ at the relevant $T_k$.

## Dispersion accounting of the estimator itself

> **Note on logic.** A calibrated estimator must carry exactly the cross-sectional spread of the posterior means —
> no more (noise masquerading as differentiation), no less (real quality differences erased). Check the books:

$$
\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\text{fwd}}\big)
= \underbrace{\rho^2\,\tau^2}_{\text{truth recovered via IS, kept in full}}
+ \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k\;\sigma^2\,\frac{T_k\,\sigma^2}{1+T_k\,\sigma^2}}_{\text{IS-invisible truth, kept in proportion to live evidence}},
$$

matching $\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\mathbb{E}[S_k \mid \text{data}]\big)$ term by term. Of the ledger's entries:
truth recovered via IS — kept in full; IS-invisible truth — admitted only to the degree each live record has resolved
it; yardstick noise — kept not at all. Shrinking further would under-disperse (underrating genuinely good
strategies); shrinking less would let noise pass as differentiation. **No additional regularization belongs**: the
dispersion books balance.

## Built-in properties

- **All shrinkage included, once.** Pull toward the book mean lives inside $(\alpha,\beta)$ (regression (B)); pull of
  noisy OOS toward the prediction lives inside the year-weights (regression (C)). Do **not** shrink again.
- **Mean preservation (exact at the pass-2 fixed point):**
  $\sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k = \sum_k \tilde w_k\,\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$ under the pass-2 weights —
  the estimator redistributes credibility across strategies, never inflates the book.
- **Limits.** $T_k \to 0$: pure debiased backtest. $T_k \to \infty$: pure live record. Crossover at
  $T_k = T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$.

## Bayesian reading

The regression estimates the conditional distribution of truth given the backtest:
$S_k \mid \hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \sim \mathcal{N}\big(\alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k,\ \sigma^2\big)$ — the
regression **is** the prior, absorbing $\bar b$ into $\alpha$, the loading $a$ into $\beta$, and
$\operatorname{Var}_w(\eta+\xi)$ into $\sigma^2$. The live record is the likelihood,
$\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \mid S_k \sim \mathcal{N}(S_k,\ 1/T_k)$ — unbiased because $b_k$ never enters the OOS
equation. The boxed formula is the conjugate posterior mean; the year-weights are the precisions.

## Extensions (same architecture, richer regression)

- **Multivariate IS:** replace $\alpha + \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ with $\alpha + \boldsymbol{\beta}^\top x_k$
  (median- and mean-Sharpe together, IS skew, $\ln n_k$ for search intensity). Predictable parts of $\xi_k$ migrate
  from noise to signal. Guard with a degrees-of-freedom correction in $\sigma^2$ and out-of-fold feature selection —
  an overfit credibility-map understates $\sigma^2$ and over-credits backtests.
- **Group structure:** per-family $(\alpha_f, \beta_f, \sigma^2_f)$ when overfitting severity differs by strategy
  type (50+ strategies per family).

## Reference implementation (skeleton)

```python
import numpy as np

def forward_sharpe(S_is, S_oos, T, n_iter=4, floor=1e-3):
    def wls(x, y, w):
        wn = w / w.sum()                            # normalized weights
        xb, yb = wn @ x, wn @ y                     # weighted means
        beta  = (wn @ ((x - xb) * (y - yb))) / (wn @ (x - xb) ** 2)
        alpha = yb - beta * xb
        eps   = y - alpha - beta * x
        return alpha, beta, wn @ eps**2, wn, xb, yb

    w = T.copy()                                    # pass 1: pilot weights ~ T_k
    for _ in range(n_iter):
        alpha, beta, res_var, wn, S_is_bar, S_oos_bar = wls(S_is, S_oos, w)
        sigma2 = max(res_var - wn @ (1.0 / T), floor)   # weighted noise subtraction
        w = 1.0 / (sigma2 + 1.0 / T)                # pass 2+: consistent weights

    T_eff = 1.0 / sigma2
    b_is  = T_eff / (T_eff + T) * beta              # deviation loadings
    b_oos = T / (T_eff + T)
    S_fwd = S_oos_bar + b_is * (S_is - S_is_bar) + b_oos * (S_oos - S_oos_bar)
    se    = 1.0 / np.sqrt(T_eff + T)

    # Diagnostics from the identification chain
    V      = wn @ (S_oos - S_oos_bar) ** 2
    tau2   = max(V - wn @ (1.0 / T), floor)         # OOS lens: truth total
    var_is = wn @ (S_is - S_is_bar) ** 2
    a      = beta * var_is / tau2                   # loading of IS lens on truth
    rho2   = a * beta                               # squared IS-truth correlation
    sigma2_check = (1.0 - rho2) * tau2              # must equal sigma2 (unit test)

    # Ablation baseline: regression (C) alone (no IS at all)
    S_fwd_C = S_oos_bar + (T * tau2) / (1.0 + T * tau2) * (S_oos - S_oos_bar)

    return S_fwd, se, dict(alpha=alpha, beta=beta, sigma2=sigma2, T_eff=T_eff,
                           b_is=b_is, b_oos=b_oos, tau2=tau2, a=a, rho2=rho2,
                           sigma2_check=sigma2_check, S_fwd_C=S_fwd_C,
                           S_is_bar=S_is_bar, S_oos_bar=S_oos_bar)
```
# Forward-Looking Sharpe Estimation via Two-Pass Weighted Regression

## Inputs

For each strategy $k = 1, \dots, K$:

| Symbol | Definition |
|---|---|
| $\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$ | $\sqrt{252}\cdot\operatorname{median}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{IS}}_k)\,/\,\operatorname{std}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{IS}}_k)$ — annualized in-sample Sharpe. Median (not mean) because selection on favorable paths creates spurious positive skew in IS pnl; the median's bounded influence immunizes against it. |
| $\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$ | $\sqrt{252}\cdot\operatorname{mean}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k)\,/\,\operatorname{std}(\text{daily pnl}^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k)$ — annualized out-of-sample Sharpe. |
| $T_k$ | Length of strategy $k$'s OOS window, **in years**. |

Unobserved: the **true Sharpe** $S_k$. All dispersions are **cross-sectional** — spread across the $K$ strategies,
not time-series variance of any one strategy's pnl. Weighted moments $\bar x_w$, $\operatorname{Var}_w$,
$\operatorname{Cov}_w$ are defined in the estimation section; every moment in this document uses the same weights as
the regression pass it belongs to.

---

# Part I — What we would do if we could see the truth

The true Sharpe $S_k$ is a property of the strategy itself. It exists independently of any measurement; IS and OOS
are two **lenses** pointed at it. If truth were observable, we would run two regressions and be done.

**(B) Truth on IS** — how to read a backtest:

$$
S_k \;=\; \alpha \;+\; \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \;+\; e_k,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}_w(e) \;=\; \sigma^2 .
$$

$\beta$ is the **attenuation coefficient**: the fraction of an IS deviation that survives into expected truth.
$\sigma^2$ is what the backtest cannot see — how much true Sharpes still differ **among strategies with identical
backtests**.

**(C) Truth on OOS** — how to read a live record:

$$
S_k \;=\; \big(1-\lambda_k\big)\,\bar S_w \;+\; \lambda_k\,\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \;+\; e^\dagger_k,
\qquad
\lambda_k = \frac{\tau^2}{\tau^2 + 1/T_k} = \frac{T_k\,\tau^2}{1+T_k\,\tau^2},
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}\big(e^\dagger_k\big) = \frac{\tau^2}{1+T_k\,\tau^2},
$$

where $\tau^2 = \operatorname{Var}_w(S)$ is the dispersion of true Sharpes. This is textbook shrinkage: a live record
is truth plus noise of known size $1/T_k$, so keep the fraction $\lambda_k$ of its deviation from the book mean.

**The forward-looking formula.** With (B) as the prior and the live record as a likelihood of known precision $T_k$,
the two combine conjugately:

$$
\boxed{\;
\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k
= \frac{\dfrac{1}{\sigma^2}\Big(\alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k\Big) + T_k\,\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k}
{\dfrac{1}{\sigma^2} + T_k},
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}\big(\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k\big) = \frac{1}{\dfrac{1}{\sigma^2} + T_k}
\;}
$$

Note the relationship to (C): the update has exactly (C)'s form with $\tau^2$ replaced by $\sigma^2$. That single
substitution is the entire value of the backtest — conditioning on IS narrows the prior dispersion from $\tau^2$ (what
you'd face knowing only that the strategy is one of yours) to $\sigma^2$ (what remains after reading its backtest),
and the live record then updates against the narrower prior. Equivalently, **(C) alone is the no-IS baseline**: set
$\sigma^2 = \tau^2$ and the formula collapses to pure shrinkage of the live record.

**Everything needed is $(\alpha, \beta, \sigma^2)$ from (B), plus $T_k$.** Defining

$$
T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} \;=\; \frac{1}{\sigma^2}
$$

turns the formula into pure time-bookkeeping — the backtest contributes $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}$ synthetic years
centered on $\alpha + \beta\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k$, the live record contributes $T_k$ actual years centered on
$\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k$, and the estimate is their year-weighted average with SE $=1/\sqrt{\text{total years}}$:

$$
\hat S^{\text{fwd}}_k
= \frac{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}}\big(\alpha + \beta\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k\big) + T_k\,\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k}
{T^{\mathrm{IS}}_{\text{eff}} + T_k}.
$$

**The problem: $S_k$ is not observable, so neither (B) nor (C) can be run.** What we can run is

**(A) OOS on IS:**

$$
\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k = \alpha_A + \beta_A\,\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k + \varepsilon_k .
$$

The rest of this document establishes that (A) delivers (B) exactly, and that (C) needs no fit at all.

---

# Part II — Theory: what each lens does to the truth

$$
\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k \;=\; S_k \;+\; b_k \;+\; \eta_k,
\qquad\qquad
\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}_k \;=\; S_k \;+\; \nu_k,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\nu_k) = \frac{1}{T_k}.
$$

- $\eta_k$ — IS sampling noise (finite backtest window, pnl randomness).
- $\nu_k$ — OOS sampling noise, variance **known**: the live window's length is the yardstick.
- $b_k$ — overfitting/selection bias, **strategy-dependent** (number of variants tried, luck of the selected path,
  parameter count, median compression for that pnl shape).
- $b_k$ appears **only in the IS equation.** The live period never passed through the selection machine, so the OOS
  lens is clean: $\mathbb{E}[\nu_k \mid S_k] = 0$. This asymmetry drives every design choice below.

## The bias is $k$-dependent — decompose it

$$
b_k \;=\; \underbrace{\bar b}_{\text{average bias}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\gamma\,\big(S_k - \bar S\big)}_{\text{truth-correlated part}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\xi_k}_{\text{idiosyncratic},\ \perp S}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}_k = \text{const} + (1+\gamma)\,S_k + \underbrace{\eta_k + \xi_k}_{\text{IS noise}} .
$$

**Selection makes $\gamma < 0$**: strategies that survived despite weak truth are exactly those whose bias was large
(they needed the luck), while strong-truth strategies survived on modest luck — so at the survival margin, bias
substitutes for quality. The IS lens therefore transmits truth at compressed scale $1+\gamma < 1$ (median compression
pushes the same way), and its dispersion

$$
\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)
= \underbrace{(1+\gamma)^2\,\tau^2}_{\text{truth content}}
+ \underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w(\eta)}_{\text{sampling noise}}
+ \underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)}_{\text{selection tax}}
$$

can fall **below** $\tau^2$ — impossible under constant bias (added noise only widens), but the expected signature of
truth-anticorrelated selection. The three entries differ economically: $\operatorname{Var}_w(\eta)$ shrinks with
backtest length; $\operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)$ — the scatter of pure luck across selected strategies — grows with search
intensity and does *not* shrink with backtest length. A longer backtest of an overfit strategy is a longer overfit
backtest.

**Nothing about $b_k$ needs to be modeled**, because the three components land in the three regression outputs:

| Component of $b_k$ | Effect on the IS lens | Absorbed by |
|---|---|---|
| $\bar b$ — average inflation | shifts the IS mean | $\alpha$ (intercept recenters onto the OOS anchor) |
| $\gamma(S_k - \bar S)$ — truth-correlated tilt | compresses the scale to $1+\gamma$ | $\beta$ (slope adapts) |
| $\xi_k$ — idiosyncratic scatter | extra IS noise | flattens $\beta$, inflates $\sigma^2$ — measured and charged to the backtest's credibility |

This is the content of "the regression is the prior": whatever tangle of bias, compression, and noise produced the IS
number, its net effect on *what IS says about truth* is captured by $(\alpha, \beta, \sigma^2)$.

---

# Part III — Recovering (B) and (C) from what we observe

## Step 1 — (A) and (B) have the same line

Both regress on the same regressor; their dependent variables differ by $\nu$, which is uncorrelated with
$\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}$:

$$
\beta_A = \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\, S + \nu\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}
= \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\, S\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}
= \beta,
\qquad \alpha_A = \alpha,
\qquad \varepsilon_k = e_k + \nu_k .
$$

**Noise in the dependent variable does not move a regression line** — it only inflates the residual. So the slope and
intercept of (B) are read directly off (A), with no correction. (This is also why the slope automatically targets
*truth* rather than the noisy OOS print.)

## Step 2 — clean the residual to get $\sigma^2$

The residual of (A) is (B)'s residual plus yardstick noise, independent parts:

$$
\operatorname{Var}_w(\varepsilon) = \sigma^2 + \sum_k \tilde w_k \frac{1}{T_k}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\boxed{\;\sigma^2 = \operatorname{Var}_w(\varepsilon) - \sum_k \tilde w_k \frac{1}{T_k}\;}
$$

This single subtraction is the whole trick: **keep (A)'s coefficients, clean (A)'s residual variance, and you have
(B).** Skipping it would blame the yardstick's shakiness on the backtest — concluding "IS is uninformative" merely
because the OOS windows are short.

## Step 3 — $\tau^2$ from the OOS lens gives (C)

The OOS lens has only two variance entries (unbiased, unit loading, known noise):

$$
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}_{V\ \text{measured}}
= \underbrace{\tau^2}_{\text{truth}} + \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k \frac{1}{T_k}}_{\text{yardstick noise}}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\tau^2 = V - \sum_k \tilde w_k \frac{1}{T_k}.
$$

So (C) requires **no fit** — its coefficients $\lambda_k = T_k\tau^2/(1+T_k\tau^2)$ are functions of two already-known
numbers. This is exactly what a clean lens buys.

## The dispersion ledger

Combining Steps 2 and 3, the measured OOS spread splits three ways:

$$
V \;=\; \underbrace{\tau^2 - \sigma^2}_{\text{truth explained by IS}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sigma^2}_{\text{truth invisible to IS}}
\;+\; \underbrace{\sum_k \tilde w_k \frac{1}{T_k}}_{\text{yardstick noise}},
$$

with the first term equal to $\beta^2 \operatorname{Var}_w(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}})$, the dispersion of (B)'s fitted
values. Two readings of explanatory power follow, and they differ:

$$
R^2_A = \frac{\tau^2 - \sigma^2}{V}\ \ \text{(observable)},
\qquad
R^2_B = 1 - \frac{\sigma^2}{\tau^2}\ \ \text{(what you want)},
\qquad
R^2_B = R^2_A \cdot \frac{V}{\tau^2} \;>\; R^2_A .
$$

**The reported $R^2$ understates the backtest** by the factor $V/\tau^2$, because its denominator counts yardstick
noise that no predictor could ever explain. The honest question is not "what fraction of $V$ does IS explain" but
"what fraction of $\tau^2$" — which is $R^2_B$.

## Attenuation, in these terms

$$
\beta = \frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}},\ \hat S^{\mathrm{OOS}}\big)}{\operatorname{Var}_w\big(\hat S^{\mathrm{IS}}\big)}
\qquad\text{(model-free — what the pipeline computes)}
$$

$$
\beta = \frac{(1+\gamma)\,\tau^2}{(1+\gamma)^2\tau^2 + \operatorname{Var}_w(\eta) + \operatorname{Var}_w(\xi)}
\qquad\text{(measurement model — where the intuition lives)}
$$

With no selection ($\gamma = 0$) the second reduces to the classical
$\beta = \tau^2/(\tau^2 + \text{noise})$ — the signal share of the backtest's dispersion. $\beta = 0.4$ then reads:
60% of the spread in your backtests carries no forward information, and regression to the mean by that fraction is
arithmetic, not pessimism. Attenuation composes — more variants tried, more path selection, shorter IS windows each
flatten $\beta$ further — which makes $\beta$ the single most informative diagnostic of a research process: the
fraction of backtest differentiation that survives contact with reality, measured on your own book. (On the OOS side,
regression (C), the classical formula is not an approximation but exact, since that lens is unbiased with known
noise.)

